every checked-in GitHub workflow file presents jobs this scan can read: true
every workflow job declares a job-level runtime ceiling, unless it calls a reusable workflow: true
every workflow job states that ceiling ahead of the steps it bounds: true

Synthetic controls run this same scanner over workflow trees differing from a legitimate one
in one respect each; refusal output is captured and matched below.

a tree whose jobs carry an expression ceiling, a plain one, and a reusable-workflow call passes: true
a job with no ceiling at all is refused, named, with GitHub's 6-hour default spelled out: true
a ceiling declared after the steps it would bound is refused as out of order: true
a ceiling that exists only inside a run block scalar leaves the job unbounded: true
a per-step ceiling is not the job's own, and the job is still refused: true
a reusable-workflow call that declares a ceiling GitHub would reject is refused too: true
a timeout-minutes key with nothing behind it declares no ceiling and is refused: true
a timeout-minutes key whose whole value is a comment is refused the same way: true
a real ceiling wearing a trailing comment is a ceiling, and its job passes: true
a reusable-workflow call carrying the key with no value is refused on the key, not the value: true
a .yaml workflow is discovered and judged like a .yml one: true
a job whose steps sequence starts at its own key indentation is still judged, and refused unbounded: true
an inline jobs value is refused as a shape this reader cannot decide: true
a jobs key with no entries under it is refused rather than read as a file of no jobs: true
an inline job body is refused, its keys being unreadable to this reader: true
a job with no keys under it is refused rather than read as a job without a ceiling: true
an unreadable line inside an otherwise valid job is refused, ceiling and all: true
the same job with that key spelled plainly is read and passes: true
a quoted job key is a job like any other, and its bounded job passes: true
the same quoted job without a ceiling is refused, so quoting hides nothing: true
a sequence entry where a job mapping belongs is refused, not skipped: true
a quoted key opens a block scalar too, its body staying content: true
a document with a YAML directive and an indented root mapping is read: true
a decoy jobs mapping inside a multiline quoted scalar is refused, never read as the real one: true
the same field written on one line, colon and quotes and all, is read normally: true
an apostrophe inside a plain scalar is text, not a quote this reader must follow: true
a quote partway through a plain scalar is text too, whitespace before it notwithstanding: true
a hyphen partway through a plain scalar indicates nothing, quotes after it included: true
keys carrying YAML's separation space before the colon are read as the keys they are: true
a leading byte-order mark does not hide the first key: true
a document-end marker leaves an indented root mapping readable: true
a tab inside a run block scalar is content, and its workflow passes: true
a block header carrying YAML's indentation and chomping indicators still opens a block: true
a root mapping the document indents uniformly is read, `jobs:` not having to sit at column zero: true
a tab-indented workflow is refused, this reader measuring indentation in spaces: true
a workflow file with no jobs mapping is refused rather than read as having no jobs: true
a YAML file nested under .github/workflows is no workflow, and its unbounded job is not judged: true

